<<<
sink, vb. 1. Past tense sank or

sunk, the former now prevailing,

especially in intransitive senses.

2. Sunk(en).
===
**sink, vb.** 1. Past tense *sank* or *sunk*, the former now prevailing, especially in intransitive senses. 2. *Sunk(en).*
>>>

<<<
compound passive verb: the ship would have been, will be, was, sunk, not sunken. But sunken has not a corresponding
===
compound passive verb: *the ship would have been, will be, was, sunk, not sunken.* But *sunken* has not a corresponding
>>>

<<<
monopoly of the adjectival uses: sunken eyes; a sunken (or sunk) rock; a sunk or sunken ship; a sunk (or sunken) fence; sunk carving; a sunk panel, shelf, storey. Roughly, sunken is used
===
monopoly of the adjectival uses: *sunken eyes; a sunken (or sunk) rock; a sunk or sunken ship; a sunk (or sunken) fence; sunk carving; a sunk panel, shelf, storey.* Roughly, *sunken* is used
>>>

<<<
the position that results from sinking, i.e. it is an INTRANSITIVE P.P.; & sunk is used of what has been sunk esp. by human agency.
===
the position that results from sinking, i. e. it is an ^^INTRANSITIVE P.P.^^; & *sunk* is used of what has been sunk esp. by human agency.
>>>

